Uploading Maps of Files with Spring Cloud OpenFeign: A Comprehensive Guide

Spring Cloud OpenFeign provides a powerful mechanism for building resilient, declarative REST clients. However, when it comes to uploading complex data structures like Map<String, MultipartFile>, things can get tricky. This article will guide you through the process of seamlessly uploading maps of files using OpenFeign, ensuring your application handles this task with grace.

Scenario: Imagine you need to upload multiple files, each associated with a specific key, using a REST endpoint. This scenario could arise in scenarios like:

  • Document Management: Uploading multiple documents (files) associated with different sections or categories.
  • Product Catalog: Uploading images for various product variations.
  • Data Analysis: Uploading raw data files categorized by specific attributes.

Let's assume you have a Spring Cloud OpenFeign client interface resembling the following:

@FeignClient(name = "file-service")
public interface FileService {

    @PostMapping("/files")
    ResponseEntity<String> uploadFiles(@RequestPart("files") Map<String, MultipartFile> files); 

The code snippet above aims to upload a Map<String, MultipartFile> to the /files endpoint of the file-service. However, directly using @RequestPart with a Map<String, MultipartFile> will often lead to issues with how OpenFeign handles multipart requests.

The Issue:

OpenFeign's default behavior is to handle @RequestPart annotations for individual parameters. This approach works well for single file uploads but falls short when handling complex structures like Map<String, MultipartFile>. The issue arises because OpenFeign tries to serialize the entire map as a single part, leading to errors on the server side.

To overcome this challenge, we need to leverage OpenFeign's flexibility by customizing its behavior for multipart uploads. This can be achieved by introducing a custom SpringEncoder and registering it with the OpenFeign client.

Here's a step-by-step guide:

  1. Custom SpringEncoder: Create a custom encoder that leverages Spring's MultipartBodyBuilder to build the multipart request.
import feign.Request;
import feign.Request.Options;
import feign.RequestTemplate;
import feign.codec.EncodeException;
import feign.codec.Encoder;
import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
import org.springframework.http.codec.multipart.MultipartBodyBuilder;
import org.springframework.util.MultiValueMap;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.BodyInserter;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.Map;

public class MultipartEncoder implements Encoder {

    private final WebClient webClient;

    public MultipartEncoder(WebClient webClient) {
        this.webClient = webClient;

    public Request encode(Object object, RequestTemplate template) throws EncodeException {
        // Ensure the object is a Map<String, MultipartFile>
        if (!(object instanceof Map)) {
            throw new EncodeException("Object is not a Map<String, MultipartFile>");

        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        Map<String, MultipartFile> files = (Map<String, MultipartFile>) object;

        // Create a multipart body builder
        MultipartBodyBuilder builder = MultipartBodyBuilder.form();
        files.forEach((key, file) -> builder.part(key, file.getResource()));

        // Build the request body
        BodyInserter<MultiValueMap<String, Object>, ? extends Mono<? extends MultiValueMap<String, Object>>> body = builder.build();
        Mono<MultiValueMap<String, Object>> bodyMono = body.insert(webClient.mutate().build());

        // Encode the request
            Request request = webClient.post()
                    .uri(template.url())
                    .contentType(MediaType.MULTIPART_FORM_DATA)
                    .body(bodyMono)
                    .toEntity(String.class)
                    .block().getBody();
            return Request.create(template.method(), template.url(), template.headers(), request, Options.DEFAULT, template.request());
        } catch (IOException e) {
            throw new EncodeException("Failed to encode request", e);
  1. Register the Custom Encoder: Add the custom encoder to the OpenFeign client configuration.
public class FeignConfig {

    public Encoder multipartEncoder(WebClient.Builder webClientBuilder) {
        return new MultipartEncoder(webClientBuilder.build());

    public Feign.Builder feignBuilder() {
        return Feign.builder()
                .encoder(new MultipartEncoder(WebClient.create()));
